# Labels: labs

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`labs()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/labs.html), which covers `xlab()`, `ylab()`,
`ggtitle()` and `get_labs()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's
code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names
what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`labs()` sets the text of a plot's labels: the title of each axis or legend, named by its aesthetic,
and the plot's title, subtitle, caption and tag. `xlab()`, `ylab()` and `ggtitle()` are older
shortcuts for some of them, and `get_labs()` returns the labels a plot ends up with. Giggly has
`Giggly.Plot.labs/2`, which sets every one of these labels; unset, an axis is titled by the column
mapped to it, or `count` for counted bars, a legend or colour bar by its column, and a plot has no
title, subtitle, caption or tag. ggplot2 has superseded `xlab()`, `ylab()` and `ggtitle()` with
`labs()`, so Giggly has `labs/2` alone. It has no `get_labs()`. A plot is described with
`Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and
shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is
`Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, which the
setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## Renaming a legend or an axis

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt, colour = cyl)) + geom_point()
p + labs(colour = "Cylinders")
p + labs(x = "New x label")
```

A label is named by the aesthetic it titles: `colour` titles the colour bar, which would otherwise
be titled `cyl`, and `x` the x axis, otherwise `mpg`.

```elixir
p =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :mpg, y: :wt, colour: :cyl})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

p
|> Plot.labs(colour: "Cylinders")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(x: "New x label")
|> render.()
```

## Labels by column

A `dictionary` relabels by column rather than by aesthetic: a label that is a column's name, here
`mpg`, `wt` or `cyl`, becomes that column's entry. The plot does not use `disp`, so its entry
changes nothing.

```r
# Set labels by variable name instead of aesthetic
p + labs(dictionary = c(
  disp = "Displacment", # Not in use
  cyl  = "Number of cylinders",
  mpg  = "Miles per gallon",
  wt   = "Weight (1000 lbs)"
))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `labs()`'s `dictionary` option.

## A title and subtitle

```r
# The plot title appears at the top-left, with the subtitle
# display in smaller text underneath it
p + labs(title = "New plot title")
p + labs(title = "New plot title", subtitle = "A subtitle")
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(title: "New plot title")
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(title: "New plot title", subtitle: "A subtitle")
|> render.()
```

## A caption

```r
# The caption appears in the bottom-right, and is often used for
# sources, notes or copyright
p + labs(caption = "(based on data from ...)")
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(caption: "(based on data from ...)")
|> render.()
```

## A tag

```r
# The plot tag appears at the top-left, and is typically used
# for labelling a subplot with a letter.
p + labs(title = "title", tag = "A")
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(title: "title", tag: "A")
|> render.()
```

## Removing a label

```r
# If you want to remove a label, set it to NULL.
p +
 labs(title = "title") +
 labs(title = NULL)
```

The second `labs()` removes the title the first one set. Giggly writes R's `NULL` as `:none`, which
also takes away the room the title took.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.labs(title: "title")
|> Plot.labs(title: :none)
|> render.()
```